Theorem DP-10-REMOVED: DP-10 removal soundness via constructive counterexample + 3-alternative-paths enumeration

  Preconditions:
    - Annex E §AIMS DP-10 REMOVED note: prior DP-10 claimed
        Owned AND Linear AND Once => RC == 1
      and was REMOVED as unsound. The removal soundness is the proof
      obligation discharged here.
    - Backward analysis proves "no future duplication" (Linear + Once) but
      NOT "no existing aliases". Uniqueness is the SSOT for RC == 1 claims
      per Annex E §AIMS; DP-10 attempted to derive uniqueness from
      Consumption + Cardinality, bypassing the Uniqueness dimension.
    - The three alternative paths (per §1.4 + TF-3 + IC-3) subsume DP-10's
      use cases without unsoundness:
        (1) Uniqueness = Unique via §1.4 -> DP-7 RC-skip-eligible
        (2) TF-3 FRESH allocation -> Uniqueness = Unique -> DP-7
        (3) IC-3 ParamContract.uniqueness = Unique -> DP-9 row (c)
            StaticUnique
    - The §01A CH-102 pattern (per
      aims-proof/proofs/01-decisions/CH-102*.proof + section_01A.rs) is
      the precedent: constructive counterexample exhibition + 3-alternative-
      paths enumeration. This proof mirrors that shape for DP-10's
      removal.

  Soundness property:
    REMOVAL claim: DP-10 (Owned AND Linear AND Once => RC == 1) is unsound
      AND every prior use case is subsumed by one of the three alternative
      paths (Uniqueness, TF-3, IC-3).

    Formally:
      (a) Exists s in AimsState canonical, satisfying (Owned AND Linear AND
          Once), with RC > 1 (counterexample to former DP-10).
      (b) Forall use-case-of-former-DP-10 c, exists path p in
          {Uniqueness-direct, TF-3-fresh, IC-3-contract} such that p
          discharges c soundly.

  Proof obligation:
    Two-part discharge:
      (a) Constructive counterexample: exhibit s satisfying former DP-10
          antecedent (Owned, Linear, Once) with RC > 1 establishing
          unsoundness.
      (b) Alternative-paths exhibition: enumerate the 3 paths and
          demonstrate per-path subsumption of former DP-10's use cases.

    Part (a) — Constructive counterexample (DP-10's unsoundness):

      Consider variable v with:
        s = (Owned, Linear, Once, MaybeShared, BlockLocal, ReusableStruct,
             {may_alloc=true})
        is_scalar(s) = false

      Backward dataflow at this point proves:
        Linear + Once => v used exactly once forward, then dies.
        Owned => v owns its allocation reference.
        Former DP-10 conclusion: RC(v) == 1.

      Counterexample to DP-10 — backward analysis CANNOT prove uniqueness:

        Suppose an EARLIER `store_into_data_structure` site at point p1
        upstream of v's current use created an alias of v via RcInc — e.g.,
        v was inserted into a list at p1, which emitted RcInc(v) to keep
        the list's reference alive. The list still holds the reference
        (RC contribution from list = 1). At v's current single Linear+Once
        use (point p2), backward analysis sees:
          - exactly one Linear use of v in the forward direction (true)
          - no future use beyond p2 (true)
          - therefore no future RcInc on v (true)
        But the EXISTING alias from p1 (the list's RcInc) is invisible to
        backward analysis. Per Annex E §AIMS: Uniqueness encodes the
        PAST guarantee "v not duplicated"; Linearity encodes the FUTURE
        guarantee "v consumed once." Former DP-10 conflated the two.

      The variable's Uniqueness = MaybeShared (NOT Unique) precisely
      captures this: the upstream RcInc at p1 prevents the FRESH+Linear
      pipeline from achieving Unique status. Per §1.4 Uniqueness = SSOT
      for RC == 1 claims; former DP-10's bypass was unsound.

      Concrete IR sketch (BUG-XX construction; consult Annex E §AIMS Return Provenance + TF-3 for AimsState assignment rules):

        # Earlier point p1 — store creates upstream alias
        %0 = Construct(List<int>, [...]) # TF-3 FRESH: (Owned, Linear,
                                              # Once, Unique, BlockLocal,
                                              # CollectionBuffer, may_alloc)
        %1 = Construct(Box<int>, [42]) # TF-3 FRESH: (Owned, Linear,
                                              # Once, Unique, BlockLocal,
                                              # ReusableStruct, may_alloc)
        RcInc(%1) # alias created — list now
                                              # holds ref; %1.Uniqueness
                                              # demotes to MaybeShared
        Set(%0[0], %1) # %1 stored into list at p1
        # ... %1 still live and Linear+Once on its remaining path ...

        # Current point p2 — DP-10 antecedent satisfied; conclusion unsound
        Apply(some_owned_consuming_callee, [%1]) # %1's Linear+Once use
                                                    # Former DP-10: RC==1
                                                    # ACTUAL: RC==2 (list +
                                                    # callee both hold)

      Former DP-10 conclusion "RC == 1" would license the callee to omit
      its dec on %1, but actual RC > 1 means the omitted dec causes a
      leak (the list's reference now refcount-skewed; the list's eventual
      drop would underflow to zero while the value is genuinely shared).

    Part (b) — 3 alternative paths (subsumption enumeration):

      Path 1: Uniqueness = Unique direct (per §1.4 -> DP-7)
        When the analysis CAN prove Uniqueness = Unique (no upstream RcInc;
        no aliasing through projections; no escape to heap), DP-7 is the
        sound RC-skip-eligibility predicate. Per Annex E §AIMS DP-7:
          is_rc_skip_eligible(s) iff is_local AND Owned AND Linear AND
          Once AND Unique AND non-scalar.
        DP-7 includes Uniqueness = Unique as load-bearing conjunct
        (see DP-7 proof, Row 5 + Shared+Linear+Once counterexample).
        Subsumption: former DP-10's "single Linear+Once use of Owned
        non-scalar value" use case maps onto DP-7 WITH the Uniqueness
        gate. Cases former DP-10 incorrectly applied to (Uniqueness =
        MaybeShared or Shared) are correctly excluded by DP-7's
        Uniqueness = Unique conjunct.

      Path 2: TF-3 FRESH allocation -> Uniqueness = Unique -> DP-7
        TF-3 (Construct) sets dst.uniqueness := Unique by construction
        (per Annex E §AIMS TF-3 + Appendix A Construct row). All fresh
        allocations start Unique; only subsequent RcInc or escape
        operations canonicalize (CN-6) Unique -> MaybeShared. When a value
        is provably fresh AND its forward usage is Linear+Once AND it
        does not escape (Locality <= FunctionLocal) AND no RcInc on the
        forward path, DP-7 applies and RC-skip is sound.
        Subsumption: former DP-10's "fresh + immediately-consumed value"
        use case maps onto TF-3 setting Unique + DP-7. The freshness
        is recorded structurally via TF-3, not derived via Consumption +
        Cardinality.

      Path 3: IC-3 ParamContract.uniqueness = Unique -> DP-9 row (c)
        StaticUnique
        When a callee parameter's IC-3 ParamContract.uniqueness = Unique
        is established by SCC fixpoint (per Annex E §AIMS IC-2 + IC-3),
        the caller's arg may be Uniqueness = MaybeShared but the
        interprocedural contract proves the callee may treat the arg as
        Unique at entry. Per Annex E §AIMS DP-9 row (c): MaybeShared
        AND IC-3.uniqueness = Unique AND can_mutate_in_place(DP-5) = true
        -> StaticUnique. This is the legitimate cross-procedural
        uniqueness path; the DP-5 joint check ensures local borrow safety
        composes with the interprocedural fixed-point.
        Subsumption: former DP-10's "caller-arg-locally-treated-as-unique"
        use case maps onto IC-3 + DP-5 + DP-9 composition. The
        interprocedural contract carries the uniqueness obligation; DP-9
        is the gate; DP-5 closes the local-borrow-safety gap.

    Three-alternatives exhaustiveness:
      The trichotomy (Path 1 direct Uniqueness, Path 2 FRESH allocation,
      Path 3 IC-3 contract) covers every legitimate use case former DP-10
      attempted to discharge:
        - Direct lattice proof of Uniqueness via Uniqueness dimension
          (Path 1).
        - Structural proof via fresh-allocation TF-3 setting Unique
          (Path 2).
        - Interprocedural proof via IC-3 ParamContract (Path 3).
      No fourth path is needed; former DP-10's antecedent (Owned + Linear
      + Once) is strictly weaker than any of the three paths (each
      adds the Uniqueness obligation explicitly), so former DP-10's
      conclusion is subsumed.

    §01A CH-102 pattern mirror:
      CH-102 (in aims-proof/proofs/01-decisions/CH-102*.proof per §01A
      `discharge_for_engine` clauses for the CoexistenceHandshake
      category) exhibits the same shape:
        (a) constructive counterexample establishing former rule's
            unsoundness;
        (b) 3-alternative-paths enumeration proving every legitimate use
            case is subsumed;
        (c) `case_analysis` PRIMARY engine via finite enumeration over
            the counterexample's lattice projection + per-path
            subsumption witness.
      This proof artifact's structure matches CH-102's per the §05.1
      Implementation Item the section-05 coverage manifest pattern-mirroring requirement.

    Live-variable / borrow_sources / project_alias_sources / IC-3 contract
    typed pre-pass dependency:
      Path 3 cites IC-3 ParamContract.uniqueness as upstream input. The
      DP-10-REMOVED soundness proof does NOT require evaluation against
      a live IC-3 ParamContract — the proof obligation is the REMOVAL,
      not the evaluation of a successor predicate. The three alternative
      paths are exhibited structurally, not evaluated against a live
      AimsStateMap. Each path's soundness is established by the
      corresponding rule's own proof artifact (DP-7 proof for Path 1+2;
      DP-9 proof at §05.2 for Path 3).

    Engines dispatched:
      case_analysis (PRIMARY — constructive counterexample enumeration
        (1 witness) + 3-alternative-paths subsumption enumeration (3
        paths) per section_05::verify_dp10_removed; the verifier checks
        the counterexample antecedent (Owned, Linear, Once) holds AND
        Uniqueness != Unique AND enumerates the 3 paths as the
        subsumption witness)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_05 case_analysis engine discharges DP-10-REMOVED via constructive counterexample (Owned + Linear + Once with MaybeShared upstream alias) + 3-alternative-paths enumeration (Uniqueness direct, TF-3 FRESH, IC-3 ParamContract) per Annex E §AIMS DP-10 REMOVED note + §1.4 + §3 TF-3 + §5 IC-3; mirrors §01A CH-102 pattern
